    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d251">

                <pb facs="0358" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1901/pg278"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hill to Mr.
                            Powell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1901-02-08"><hi rendition="#italic">February 8,
                        1901</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 452.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your No. 822, of October 10,
                    last, relative to the alleged right of the Haitian Government to impose higher
                    duties on United States vessels carrying French goods than on French vessels, I
                    have to say that you may advise the Government of Haiti that this Government is
                    of opinion that Article X of the convention of 1864 between the United States
                    and Haiti is quite independent of Article XI and creates absolute rights, which
                    this Government can not fail to insist upon. Should, therefore, any higher
                    charges be collected on American tonnage than that of any other country they
                    will be reclaimed.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">David J. Hill</hi>,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Acting Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
